{"task_id": "Mbpp/468", "solution": "def generate_subsequences(arr):\n    \"\"\"Generates all possible non-empty subsequences from the input array while maintaining relative order.\"\"\"\n    all_subsequences = []\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    def backtrack(current_subsequence, start_index):\n        if current_subsequence:\n            all_subsequences.append(list(current_subsequence))\n\n        for i in range(start_index, n):\n            current_subsequence.append(arr[i])\n            backtrack(current_subsequence, i + 1)\n            current_subsequence.pop() # Backtrack\n\n    backtrack([], 0)\n    return all_subsequences\n\ndef is_increasing(subseq):\n    \"\"\"Validates if a subsequence is strictly increasing.\"\"\"\n    if len(subseq) <= 1:\n        return True\n    for i in range(1, len(subseq)):\n        if subseq[i] <= subseq[i-1]:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef calculate_product(subseq):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the product of all elements in a subsequence.\"\"\"\n    product = 1\n    for num in subseq:\n        product *= num\n    return product\n\ndef find_maximum(products):\n    \"\"\"Finds the maximum value from a list of products, returning 0 if the list is empty.\"\"\"\n    if not products:\n        return 0\n    return max(products)\n\ndef max_product(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product formed by multiplying numbers of an increasing subsequence of that array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    all_subsequences = generate_subsequences(arr)\n    \n    products_of_increasing_subsequences = []\n    for subseq in all_subsequences:\n        if is_increasing(subseq):\n            products_of_increasing_subsequences.append(calculate_product(subseq))\n    \n    # If no increasing subsequences (e.g., all numbers are negative or empty input, handled by find_maximum)\n    # The problem context implies positive numbers, so there will always be at least single-element subsequences.\n    return find_maximum(products_of_increasing_subsequences)"}
