{"task_id": "Mbpp/799", "solution": "def left_rotate(number: int, d: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rotates the bits of a 32-bit number to the left by d positions.\n    Overflow bits wrap around to the right side of the number.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Ensure d is within the range [0, 31] for 32-bit rotation\n    # A rotation by 32 bits is equivalent to no rotation, so d % 32 is sufficient.\n    d = d % 32\n    \n    # Left shift the number by d bits\n    shifted_left = number << d\n    \n    # Extract the bits that overflowed past the 32nd bit\n    # These are the bits from the (32+d-1) down to the 32nd position.\n    overflow_bits = shifted_left >> 32\n    \n    # Mask to keep only the lower 32 bits of the left-shifted number\n    # This effectively \"truncates\" the number to 32 bits, losing the overflow.\n    truncated_shifted_left = shifted_left & 0xFFFFFFFF\n    \n    # Combine the truncated left-shifted part with the overflow bits\n    # The overflow bits are placed at the least significant positions.\n    result = truncated_shifted_left | overflow_bits\n    \n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/801", "solution": "def test_three_equal(param1: int, param2: int, param3: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Count the number of equal values among three integers.\"\"\"\n    if param1 == param2 and param2 == param3:\n        return 3\n    elif param1 == param2 or param1 == param3 or param2 == param3:\n        return 2\n    else:\n        return 0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/803", "solution": "import math\n\ndef is_perfect_square(number: [int, float]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given number is a perfect square.\n    Handles negative numbers and floating-point precision.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        return False\n    \n    if not isinstance(number, (int, float)):\n        # As per design context, no isinstance() checks or exceptions,\n        # but the type hint implies numeric input. Returning False for non-numeric.\n        return False\n\n    # Calculate square root with floating-point precision\n    sqrt_val = math.isqrt(int(number)) if number == int(number) else math.sqrt(number)\n    \n    # Check if the square root is an integer (within a small tolerance for floats)\n    # The design context mentions O(log n) efficiency for square root calculation,\n    # math.isqrt handles integers efficiently. For floats, standard math.sqrt is used.\n    # The comparison against itself squared helps handle precision issues.\n    return abs(sqrt_val * sqrt_val - number) < 1e-9 and (sqrt_val == int(sqrt_val))"}
